  "ex_pecho_6_name": "Incline Barbell Press",
  "ex_pecho_6_desc": "Set the bench to 30-45°. Grip the bar slightly wider than shoulder width. Lower the bar in a controlled manner to your upper chest, then press up to full extension. Keep your lower back against the bench and your shoulder blades retracted.",
  "ex_pecho_6_b1": "Emphasizes the upper chest (clavicular portion)",
  "ex_pecho_6_b2": "Allows heavier loads than dumbbells",
  "ex_pecho_6_b3": "Improves pressing strength from elevated angles",
  "ex_pecho_7_name": "Decline Dumbbell Press",
  "ex_pecho_7_desc": "Set the bench to a 15-30° decline. With a dumbbell in each hand at chest height, press up, bringing the dumbbells together at the top. Lower with control, feeling the stretch in the lower portion of your chest.",
  "ex_pecho_7_b1": "Works the lower portion of the chest",
  "ex_pecho_7_b2": "Reduces shoulder involvement",
  "ex_pecho_7_b3": "Greater range of motion than the decline barbell press",
  "ex_pecho_8_name": "Push-ups",
  "ex_pecho_8_desc": "Place your hands on the floor shoulder-width apart with your feet together. Keep your body straight from head to heels. Lower by bending your elbows until your chest nearly touches the floor, then push back up. Keep your core tight throughout the movement.",
  "ex_pecho_8_b1": "No equipment required",
  "ex_pecho_8_b2": "Multiple variations to progress (diamond, decline, clap)",
  "ex_pecho_8_b3": "Compound exercise that also strengthens the core",
  "ex_pecho_9_name": "Dumbbell Pullover",
  "ex_pecho_9_desc": "Lie on a flat bench holding one dumbbell with both hands over your chest, arms extended. Lower the dumbbell in an arc over your head until you feel a stretch in your chest and lats. Return to the starting position by contracting your chest.",
  "ex_pecho_9_b1": "Works the chest and lats at the same time",
  "ex_pecho_9_b2": "Improves shoulder and rib cage mobility",
  "ex_pecho_9_b3": "Excellent complement between sets of presses and rows",
  "ex_esp_6_name": "T-Bar Row",
  "ex_esp_6_desc": "Stand over the T-bar with your feet shoulder-width apart. Lean forward keeping a neutral back and pull the handles toward your chest, driving your elbows back. Squeeze your shoulder blades at the end position and lower with control.",
  "ex_esp_6_b1": "Develops mid-back thickness",
  "ex_esp_6_b2": "Allows very heavy loads with less lower back stress than the free-weight row",
  "ex_esp_6_b3": "Works posture and scapular stability",
  "ex_esp_7_name": "Seated Cable Row",
  "ex_esp_7_desc": "Sit facing the low pulley with your feet braced. Grip the attachment with your arms extended and your back neutral. Pull your hands toward your abdomen by retracting your shoulder blades and keeping your torso upright. Return in a controlled manner without rounding your back.",
  "ex_esp_7_b1": "Constant tension throughout the movement",
  "ex_esp_7_b2": "Easy weight progression and accessible technique",
  "ex_esp_7_b3": "Excellent for developing mid-back thickness",
  "ex_esp_8_name": "Dumbbell Shrugs",
  "ex_esp_8_desc": "Stand with a dumbbell in each hand at your sides, arms relaxed. Raise your shoulders toward your ears without bending your elbows. Hold the contraction for 1 second at the top and lower slowly. Don't roll your shoulders; the movement is purely vertical.",
  "ex_esp_8_b1": "Direct isolation of the upper traps",
  "ex_esp_8_b2": "Improves posture and the appearance of the neck",
  "ex_esp_8_b3": "Strengthens scapular stability for other lifts",
  "ex_esp_9_name": "Good Morning",
  "ex_esp_9_desc": "Place the bar across your traps as in a squat. With your feet hip-width apart and knees slightly bent, lean forward from the hips keeping a neutral back. Lower until your torso is nearly parallel to the floor, then return squeezing your glutes.",
  "ex_esp_9_b1": "Strengthens the posterior chain and lower back",
  "ex_esp_9_b2": "Improves the hip hinge for the deadlift and squat",
  "ex_esp_9_b3": "Key accessory work for powerlifting",
  "ex_pier_6_name": "Front Squat",
  "ex_pier_6_desc": "Rest the bar on your front delts, elbows high, with a cross-arm or clean grip. Feet shoulder-width apart. Lower keeping your torso upright and your elbows pointing forward until your thighs go below parallel. Drive through your heels to return to the starting position.",
  "ex_pier_6_b1": "Emphasizes the quads more than the back squat",
  "ex_pier_6_b2": "Demands a very strong core and upright posture",
  "ex_pier_6_b3": "Technical foundation for the clean and thruster in CrossFit",
  "ex_pier_7_name": "Bulgarian Split Squat",
  "ex_pier_7_desc": "Place the top of your back foot on a bench, holding a dumbbell in each hand. With your front foot stepped forward, lower by bending your front knee until your thigh is parallel to the floor. Push through your front heel to come back up.",
  "ex_pier_7_b1": "Unilateral work that corrects imbalances between legs",
  "ex_pier_7_b2": "Intense glute and quad activation",
  "ex_pier_7_b3": "Improves balance and hip stability",
  "ex_pier_8_name": "Romanian Deadlift",
  "ex_pier_8_desc": "Stand with the bar at hip height, using an overhand grip at shoulder width. With your knees slightly bent and fixed, slide the bar down your legs by hinging your torso from the hips until you feel tension in your hamstrings. Return squeezing your glutes.",
  "ex_pier_8_b1": "Intense isolation of the hamstrings and glutes",
  "ex_pier_8_b2": "Strengthens the posterior chain and improves the hip hinge",
  "ex_pier_8_b3": "Reduces the risk of hamstring injuries when running or jumping",
  "ex_pier_9_name": "Leg Extension",
  "ex_pier_9_desc": "Sit in the machine with your back supported and the roller on top of your instep. Extend your knees, lifting the weight until your legs are nearly straight, without locking your knees. Squeeze your quads at the top and lower with control.",
  "ex_pier_9_b1": "Direct isolation of the quads",
  "ex_pier_9_b2": "Useful for rehabilitation and targeted hypertrophy work",
  "ex_pier_9_b3": "Precise, low-risk load adjustment",
  "ex_pier_10_name": "Calf Raise",
  "ex_pier_10_desc": "Place your shoulders under the pads of the calf raise machine (or hold a dumbbell while standing). Rest the balls of your feet on a platform with your heels hanging off. Rise by pushing through the balls of your feet and squeeze your calves at the top. Lower slowly, feeling the stretch.",
  "ex_pier_10_b1": "Isolation of the soleus and gastrocnemius",
  "ex_pier_10_b2": "Improves power in jumps and sprints",
  "ex_pier_10_b3": "Reduces the risk of ankle and Achilles tendon injuries",
  "ex_hom_5_name": "Dumbbell Shoulder Press",
  "ex_hom_5_desc": "Seated with an upright backrest, hold a dumbbell at each side of your shoulders, palms facing forward. Press the dumbbells up until they nearly touch overhead, without locking your elbows. Lower with control to the starting position.",
  "ex_hom_5_b1": "Greater range of motion than the barbell press",
  "ex_hom_5_b2": "Allows each side to work independently",
  "ex_hom_5_b3": "Less shoulder stress than the barbell",
  "ex_hom_6_name": "Front Raises",
  "ex_hom_6_desc": "Stand with a dumbbell in each hand in front of your thighs, palms facing your body. Raise your arms in front of you, alternating or simultaneously, to shoulder height, keeping your elbows almost straight. Lower slowly without swinging.",
  "ex_hom_6_b1": "Isolation of the front delts",
  "ex_hom_6_b2": "Enhances the front appearance of the shoulder",
  "ex_hom_6_b3": "Accessory work for the bench press and overhead press",
  "ex_hom_7_name": "Dumbbell Reverse Fly",
  "ex_hom_7_desc": "Lean forward from the hips with your torso nearly parallel to the floor, a dumbbell in each hand and your arms hanging. Raise your arms laterally to shoulder height, elbows slightly bent. Squeeze the rear delts at the top and lower with control.",
  "ex_hom_7_b1": "Isolation of the rear delts, a key area for posture",
  "ex_hom_7_b2": "Counteracts the effects of pressing work",
  "ex_hom_7_b3": "Reduces the risk of rotator cuff injuries",
  "ex_bra_6_name": "Preacher Curl",
  "ex_bra_6_desc": "Sit at the preacher bench with your arms resting on the pad. Grip the bar (straight or EZ) with an underhand grip. Bend your elbows, bringing the bar toward your shoulders while controlling the movement. Lower slowly without locking your elbows at the bottom.",
  "ex_bra_6_b1": "Isolates the bicep by immobilizing the shoulder",
  "ex_bra_6_b2": "Makes it impossible to use momentum, strict technique",
  "ex_bra_6_b3": "Excellent for peak bicep contraction",
  "ex_bra_7_name": "Concentration Curl",
  "ex_bra_7_desc": "Sit on a bench with your legs apart. Rest the elbow of your working arm against the inside of your thigh, with the dumbbell hanging. Bend your elbow, bringing the dumbbell toward your shoulder and isolating the bicep. Squeeze at the top and lower with control.",
  "ex_bra_7_b1": "Pure bicep isolation with maximum contraction",
  "ex_bra_7_b2": "Lets you focus on the mind-muscle connection",
  "ex_bra_7_b3": "Ideal as the final exercise of an arm workout",
  "ex_bra_8_name": "Close-Grip Bench Press",
  "ex_bra_8_desc": "Lie on a flat bench and grip the bar with a close grip at shoulder width. Lower the bar in a controlled manner until it lightly touches your sternum, keeping your elbows tucked to your body. Press up, emphasizing the tricep extension.",
  "ex_bra_8_b1": "Compound exercise that emphasizes the triceps",
  "ex_bra_8_b2": "Allows heavier loads than a tricep isolation exercise",
  "ex_bra_8_b3": "Carries over to bench press and overhead press strength",
  "ex_bra_9_name": "Bench Dips",
  "ex_bra_9_desc": "Place your hands on a bench behind you with your fingers pointing forward. Extend your legs in front of you, resting on your heels (or bend your knees to make it easier). Lower by bending your elbows backward to 90°, then push back up.",
  "ex_bra_9_b1": "Tricep isolation with no equipment needed",
  "ex_bra_9_b2": "Easy to progress by adding weight on your lap",
  "ex_bra_9_b3": "Improves pressing strength from positions behind the body",
  "ex_core_5_name": "Russian Twists",
  "ex_core_5_desc": "Sit on the floor with your knees bent and your feet raised or resting on the floor, and lean back 45°. Clasp your hands together (or hold a weight) and rotate your torso from side to side, touching the floor or bringing the weight to each side. Keep your core tight.",
  "ex_core_5_b1": "Direct isolation of the obliques",
  "ex_core_5_b2": "Improves torso rotation, key in sports",
  "ex_core_5_b3": "Can be progressed by adding weight (dumbbell, plate, ball)",
  "ex_core_6_name": "Mountain Climbers",
  "ex_core_6_desc": "Get into a high plank position with your hands under your shoulders. Drive one knee toward your chest, alternating quickly, as if running in place. Keep your hips low and your core tight throughout the movement.",
  "ex_core_6_b1": "Combines core work with cardio",
  "ex_core_6_b2": "No equipment required, ideal for HIIT",
  "ex_core_6_b3": "Improves coordination and endurance",
  "ex_core_7_name": "Hollow Hold",
  "ex_core_7_desc": "Lie on your back with your arms extended overhead and your legs straight. Lift your arms and legs slightly off the floor, pressing your lower back into the floor. Hold the boat position with your core fully contracted.",
  "ex_core_7_b1": "Gymnastics foundation for many CrossFit movements",
  "ex_core_7_b2": "Strengthens the entire core isometrically",
  "ex_core_7_b3": "Improves posture and strength for strict pull-ups",
  "ex_core_8_name": "Side Plank",
  "ex_core_8_desc": "Lie on your side resting on your forearm with your elbow under your shoulder. Lift your hips off the floor, forming a straight line from your feet to your head. Hold the position without letting your hips sag. Repeat on the other side.",
  "ex_core_8_b1": "Intense isolation of the obliques and lateral core",
  "ex_core_8_b2": "Improves hip and spine stability",
  "ex_core_8_b3": "No equipment and low impact",
  "ex_core_9_name": "Dead Bug",
  "ex_core_9_desc": "Lie on your back with your arms extended toward the ceiling and your knees bent at 90° over your hips. Simultaneously lower your right arm behind your head and your left leg, extended, toward the floor. Return and alternate sides, keeping your lower back pressed into the floor.",
  "ex_core_9_b1": "Improves anti-extension core stability",
  "ex_core_9_b2": "Excellent for beginners and lower back rehabilitation",
  "ex_core_9_b3": "Works contralateral coordination",
  "ex_cf_1_name": "Clean",
  "ex_cf_1_desc": "From the floor, grip the bar with an overhand grip just outside your knees. Pull explosively by extending your hips and knees, shrug your shoulders and pull yourself under the bar, receiving it in a front squat with your elbows high. Stand up to complete the lift.",
  "ex_cf_1_b1": "Full Olympic lift for explosive power",
  "ex_cf_1_b2": "Develops coordination, speed, and strength at the same time",
  "ex_cf_1_b3": "Foundation of many CrossFit WODs",
  "ex_cf_2_name": "Power Clean",
  "ex_cf_2_desc": "Same as the Clean but without receiving the bar in a full squat: you catch the bar in a quarter squat with your elbows high and the bar in the front rack on your shoulders. Faster and less demanding on mobility than the Clean.",
  "ex_cf_2_b1": "Develops explosive power in the posterior chain",
  "ex_cf_2_b2": "Technically more accessible than the full Clean",
  "ex_cf_2_b3": "Excellent for beginners in the Olympic lifts",
  "ex_cf_3_name": "Snatch",
  "ex_cf_3_desc": "From the floor, grip the bar with a very wide grip. Pull explosively and receive the bar overhead with your arms extended in a deep squat (overhead squat). Stand up keeping the bar overhead.",
  "ex_cf_3_b1": "The most complete and technically demanding Olympic lift",
  "ex_cf_3_b2": "Develops power, mobility, and stability at the same time",
  "ex_cf_3_b3": "Burns more calories than almost any other exercise",
  "ex_cf_4_name": "Power Snatch",
  "ex_cf_4_desc": "Same as the Snatch but receiving the bar in a quarter squat with your arms extended overhead. Faster and less demanding on ankle and hip mobility.",
  "ex_cf_4_b1": "More accessible than the full Snatch",
  "ex_cf_4_b2": "Develops explosive power in the posterior chain",
  "ex_cf_4_b3": "Excellent for mobility and coordination",
  "ex_cf_5_name": "Thruster",
  "ex_cf_5_desc": "Hold the bar in the front rack position (on your front delts). Perform a full front squat and, as you come up, use the drive from your legs to press the bar overhead with your arms extended. Lower the bar to the front rack position and repeat.",
  "ex_cf_5_b1": "Works the entire body in a single movement",
  "ex_cf_5_b2": "Signature exercise of WODs like Fran",
  "ex_cf_5_b3": "Combines strength and metabolic conditioning",
  "ex_cf_6_name": "Push Press",
  "ex_cf_6_desc": "With the bar in the front rack position on your shoulders, perform a quick dip by slightly bending your knees. Extend your legs explosively to drive the bar overhead until your arms are extended. Lower with control to the starting position.",
  "ex_cf_6_b1": "Lets you move more weight than the strict overhead press",
  "ex_cf_6_b2": "Develops vertical pressing power",
  "ex_cf_6_b3": "Carries over to the Jerk and other Olympic lifts",
  "ex_cf_7_name": "Push Jerk",
  "ex_cf_7_desc": "Same as the Push Press, but as you drive the bar up, you rebend your legs to receive the bar in a quarter squat with your arms already extended. Then stand up to complete the lift. Allows heavier loads than the Push Press.",
  "ex_cf_7_b1": "Moves more weight overhead than any press",
  "ex_cf_7_b2": "Technical, high-power Olympic lift",
  "ex_cf_7_b3": "Component of the Clean & Jerk",
  "ex_cf_8_name": "Wall Balls",
  "ex_cf_8_desc": "Stand facing a wall with a medicine ball in front of your chest. Perform a deep squat and, as you come up, throw the ball at a high target on the wall (~3 m). Catch the ball at chest height and descend immediately into the next squat.",
  "ex_cf_8_b1": "Cardio and power exercise at the same time",
  "ex_cf_8_b2": "Standard movement in many CrossFit benchmarks",
  "ex_cf_8_b3": "Works coordination and rhythm",
  "ex_cf_9_name": "American Kettlebell Swing",
  "ex_cf_9_desc": "Stand with the kettlebell hanging between your legs, held with both hands. Hinge at the hips to swing it back, then extend your hips explosively to drive it overhead with your arms extended. The power comes from the hips, not the arms.",
  "ex_cf_9_b1": "Develops explosive hip power",
  "ex_cf_9_b2": "Cardio and strength in a single movement",
  "ex_cf_9_b3": "Standard in CrossFit (Russian swings only go to chest height)",
  "ex_cf_10_name": "Russian Kettlebell Swing",
  "ex_cf_10_desc": "Identical to the American swing, but the kettlebell only rises to chest/eye height, not overhead. Technically easier and less stressful on the shoulders.",
  "ex_cf_10_b1": "Entry point to kettlebell training",
  "ex_cf_10_b2": "Less shoulder stress than the American swing",
  "ex_cf_10_b3": "Ideal for beginners and high reps",
  "ex_cf_11_name": "Box Jumps",
  "ex_cf_11_desc": "Facing a sturdy box or platform, bend your knees and jump onto the box, landing with your feet fully on the surface. Extend your hips and knees at the top. Jump or step down one foot at a time to reduce impact.",
  "ex_cf_11_b1": "Pure plyometrics for developing power",
  "ex_cf_11_b2": "Improves vertical jump and explosiveness",
  "ex_cf_11_b3": "Classic component of many WODs",
  "ex_cf_12_name": "Burpees",
  "ex_cf_12_desc": "From standing, squat down and place your hands on the floor. Jump your feet back into a plank position. Perform a push-up, touching your chest to the floor. Jump your feet back toward your hands and jump straight up, extending your arms overhead.",
  "ex_cf_12_b1": "Full-body exercise with no equipment",
  "ex_cf_12_b2": "Intense cardio and strength work at the same time",
  "ex_cf_12_b3": "Standard in benchmarks like Murph and many WODs",
  "ex_cf_13_name": "Toes-to-Bar",
  "ex_cf_13_desc": "Hang from the bar with an overhand grip. Engage your lats and raise your legs, straight (or slightly bent), until your feet touch the bar. Lower with control without excessive swinging. Use the kip to keep your cadence.",
  "ex_cf_13_b1": "Highly demanding dynamic core work",
  "ex_cf_13_b2": "Improves grip strength and the lats",
  "ex_cf_13_b3": "Component of many CrossFit WODs",
  "ex_cf_14_name": "Knees-to-Elbows",
  "ex_cf_14_desc": "Hang from the bar with an overhand grip. Flex your hips and knees, bringing your knees toward your elbows in a controlled, dynamic movement. Lower with control. Useful as a progression toward Toes-to-Bar.",
  "ex_cf_14_b1": "Progression toward Toes-to-Bar",
  "ex_cf_14_b2": "Dynamic core work that is less technically demanding",
  "ex_cf_14_b3": "Improves grip strength",
  "ex_cf_15_name": "Muscle-Up",
  "ex_cf_15_desc": "Hang from the rings (or bar) with a false grip. Pull your body up explosively, transitioning over the rings, and at the top press by extending your arms until you are in the support position. Lower by controlling the reverse transition.",
  "ex_cf_15_b1": "Advanced gymnastics movement combining pulling and pushing",
  "ex_cf_15_b2": "Key milestone in CrossFit (Rx in many WODs)",
  "ex_cf_15_b3": "Develops extreme relative strength to bodyweight",
  "ex_cf_16_name": "Pistol Squat",
  "ex_cf_16_desc": "Standing on one leg, extend the other in front of you parallel to the floor. Lower into a deep squat, keeping your balance and your supporting foot flat. Rise by driving through your heel without letting the free leg touch the floor. Repeat on the other side.",
  "ex_cf_16_b1": "The most difficult single-leg squat",
  "ex_cf_16_b2": "Develops ankle and hip mobility",
  "ex_cf_16_b3": "Unilateral work that corrects asymmetries",
  "ex_cf_17_name": "Handstand Push-Up (HSPU)",
  "ex_cf_17_desc": "Place your hands on the floor about 30 cm from a wall and kick up into a handstand against the wall. Lower by bending your elbows until your head touches the floor (or an AbMat). Press to return to the top. Use the kip if you need assistance.",
  "ex_cf_17_b1": "Vertical press with your full bodyweight",
  "ex_cf_17_b2": "Improves shoulder and core stability",
  "ex_cf_17_b3": "Key gymnastics milestone in CrossFit",
  "ex_cf_18_name": "Turkish Get-Up",
  "ex_cf_18_desc": "Lie on your back holding a kettlebell with one arm extended toward the ceiling. Stand up step by step (elbow → hand → hip hinge → leg sweep → kneeling → standing), keeping the kettlebell overhead at all times. Reverse the movement.",
  "ex_cf_18_b1": "Unique total-body functional movement",
  "ex_cf_18_b2": "Improves mobility, stability, and coordination",
  "ex_cf_18_b3": "Excellent shoulder and core work",
  "ex_cf_19_name": "Double Unders",
  "ex_cf_19_desc": "Jump rope, passing the rope under your feet twice on each jump. Keep your jumps low and your wrists turning fast. Arms tucked to your body, elbows close to your waist. Requires coordination and a speed rope.",
  "ex_cf_19_b1": "Explosive, high-intensity cardio",
  "ex_cf_19_b2": "Improves coordination and timing",
  "ex_cf_19_b3": "Standard in hundreds of CrossFit WODs",
  "ex_cf_20_name": "Farmer's Carry",
  "ex_cf_20_desc": "Hold a heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in each hand at your sides. Walk with short steps, keeping an upright posture, shoulders back and core tight. Cover a set distance or work for time.",
  "ex_cf_20_b1": "Strengthens the grip and forearms",
  "ex_cf_20_b2": "Functional full-body work",
  "ex_cf_20_b3": "Improves posture and core stability",
  "ex_cf_21_name": "Goblet Squat",
  "ex_cf_21_desc": "Hold a kettlebell (or dumbbell) in front of your chest at sternum height, gripping it by the horns. Perform a deep squat, keeping your torso upright and your elbows pointing forward. Drive through your heels to stand back up.",
  "ex_cf_21_b1": "Perfect entry point to the loaded squat",
  "ex_cf_21_b2": "Teaches upright posture and proper depth",
  "ex_cf_21_b3": "Excellent for activating the core",
